    "content": "PICC Line Placement\n\n### Sample Report 1\n\n**PREOPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS:**\n1) CNS Lymphoma with need for Chemo\n2) need for IV access for chemo\n\n**POSTOPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS:**\nSame\n\n**PROCEDURE PERFORMED:**\n1) ultrasound guided L brachial vein\n2) selection for the SVC\n3) placement of a dual lumen PICC (Bard Power Line)\n\n**SURGEON:** Oliver Aalami, MD\n**ASSISTANT:**\n**ANESTHESIA:** local + sedation\n\n**ESTIMATED BLOOD LOSS:** Minimal\n**FLUIDS:** None\n**SPECIMENS:** None\n**DRAINS:** None\n**COMPLICATIONS:** None\n\n**DESCRIPTION OF PROCEDURE:**\nAfter the administration of sedation the patient's LUE was prepped and draped in the usual fashion. The LUE mid upper arm brachial vein was identified with ultrasound and noted to be fully compressible with no intrinsic echos. 1% lidocaine was used to infiltrate the planned site of access and a micropuncture needle was used to access the brachial vein. We advanced a micropuncture wire and a micropuncture sheath. Next we placed a 5Fr sheath after placing a j wire. Next we advanced a glide wire selecting for the superior vena cava through a glide catheter. Next we trimmed the Bard Power Line catheter so the tip wound end up in the SVC and removed the sheath and placed our peel away sheath and advanced the catheter over the wire. Both ports withdrew blood easily and were flushed with high dose heparin. The catheter was secured and the patient was brought to the recovery room with vital signs stable after the site was dressed.\n\nThe PICC line is ready to be used and the tip position was confirmed to be in the SVC.\n\n**DISPOSITION:**\nPatient transferred to recovery in stable condition\n\n---"
