    "title": "IVC Filter Placement",
    "content": "IVC Filter Placement\n\n### Sample Report 1\n\n**PREOPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS:**\n1) Acute DVT\n2) PE\n3) Contraindication for anticoagulation\n\n**POSTOPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS:**\nSame\n\n**PROCEDURE PERFORMED:**\n1) Ultrasound guided R common femoral vein access\n2) IVC venogram\n3) Placement of Denali Bard IVC filter\n\n**SURGEON:** Oliver Aalami, MD\n**ASSISTANT:** None\n**ANESTHESIA:** Local anesthesia with sedation\n\n**ESTIMATED BLOOD LOSS:** 5 mL\n**FLUIDS:** None\n**SPECIMENS:** None\n**DRAINS:** None\n**COMPLICATIONS:** None\n**URINE OUTPUT:** None\n\n**FINDINGS:**\nThe CFV and IVC were patent with no thrombus. The Renal vein was patent.\n\n**DESCRIPTION OF PROCEDURE:**\nAfter the right groin was prepped and draped in the usual fashion ultrasound evaluation of the R common femoral vein showed the vein to be fully compressible with no intrinsic echos. 1% lidocaine plain was infiltrated at the planned site of vein access and a micropuncture needle was used to access the right common femoral vein using ultrasound guidance. The micro-puncture wire was advanced into the common femoral vein and a nick was made in the skin with an 11 blade at the wire entrance site. The micropuncture wire and inner dilator were removed and a short angled Storq wire was advanced into the IVC using fluoroscopic guidance. We then advanced the included sheath into the IVC with the tip in the distal IVC.\n\nNext we performed an IVC venogram showing a widely patent IVC and L renal vein. The sheath was advanced to the level of the left renal vein and the Denali IVC filter was placed and delivered such that the proximal hook was right at the level of the left renal vein.\n\nA final shot confirmed placement of the IVC filter with no significant kinking of the filter noted.\n\nThe sheath was removed and manual compression was held over the access site before being dressed with gauze and a bandage. The patient was brought to the recovery room with vital signs stable.\n\n**DISPOSITION:**\nPatient transferred to recovery in stable condition\n\n---"
